    {
      "raw_rank": 8,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00081",
      "distance": 0.8365039229393005,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 140,
      "page_end": 141,
      "text": "For this reason, I prefer the metaphor of the ambient. What is ambient is immersive, osmotic, peripheral. Ambience is not so link driven, for it suggests many tactile forms besides connections among already established points or nodes. The link gives us little leeway with the more ephemeral, nuratic modalities of everyday life. Indeed, the network is ultimately still invested in binaries (movement and node, line and point), even if such binaries are wrenched by the strange loops of emergent behavior. The richly osmotic character of ambience includes choric engagement and interaction beyond the link. As I will address in the next chapter, this includes affect: affect certainly circulates in and gives rise to networks, but we need to augment this understanding of affect as more fundamental, being the mood, or affective comportment, from which our attitudes, decisions, and actions emerge. The attunement to what is choric reminds us that we cannot reduce the world to simple presence, to what is already revealed. but must rather attend to genesis, how things come to be as they are. The concept of ambience is better suited to gathering kairos and logos into a worldliness that includes but also transcends the human, showing how they take part in us even as they will us, speak us.\n\nBut how, exactly, do kairos and logos pick out various ways in which discourse and environs come to speak or will us? What can show this to be something other than a return to the subject/object split, which privileges\n\nan objective, exterior environment over a subject? As I have previously argued. subject and object derive from an originary belongingness, and if we want to keep these terms, we need to see them more as a \"mutual taking part in.\" But insofar as this \"taking part in\" is ultimately ambient, it simultaneously includes and exceeds the \"subject\"; only in this sense can we understand the kairotic environs as willing us. Nor, as should become clear, am I merely collapsing distinct realms into an abstract unity; this is equally impossible, for the ambient, if thought alongside complexity theory, can be seen as an emergent level of order discontinuous from what is given by the ensemble of original individual elements. Rather than possess some a priori ontological status, distinctions emerge dynamically and fluidly within the situation and are ultimately only an additional modality in the overall ambience. As I began this chapter with Taylor's meditation on a writer writing, I want to once again consider what happens as a writer writes. But I begin doing so by going back before Taylor.\n\nBarrett J. Mandel's 1980 essay \"The Writer Writing Is Not at Home\" stands as remarkably prescient for my purposes, and it makes an illuminating contrast with Taylor's work. Mandel opens his essay with a snippet from an interview with the American painter Jasper Johns, conducted, oddly enough, by Michael Crichton:\n\nThe author [Crichton] asks Johns why he has just made a change in the handle of a spoon in a lithograph on which he is working. Johns answers, \"Because I did.\" The author asks, \"But what did you see?\" Johns: '·I saw that it should be changed.\" Author: \"Well, if you changed it, what was wrong with it before?\" Johns: \"Nothing. I tend to think one thing is as good as another.\" Author: \"Then why change it?\" Johns, after a sigh and a pause: \"Well, I may change it again.\" Author: \"Why?\" Johns: \"Well, I won't know until I doit.\" (370)\n\nThis is high comedy, no doubt, with Crichton coming off as a clueless interviewer. 19 Still, Mandel notes the difficulties in communication and defends Johns as being as honest as he can. The artist, Mandel argues, creates \"out ofno prior knowledge, thought, plan, or expectations\" (370). Plans and thoughts do come into play, but \"the work of art does not arise from them; they do not cause the work of art to materialize\" (370). Ann Berthoff makes a similar point when she snipes about a grad student who writes something down, erases, writes again, erases, and never gets past the blank sheet. 20\n\nMy point here should be obvious: the work of art, like the work of writing",
      "meta": {
        "content_type": "text",
